            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Building on Existing Classes</h2>
                    <p>In the previous lesson, you learned how to create classes. Now we'll explore two powerful OOP concepts: <strong>Inheritance</strong> (creating new classes based on existing ones) and <strong>Polymorphism</strong> (treating objects of different types uniformly).</p>
                    
                    <p>These concepts help you write more reusable, maintainable, and flexible code.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Inheritance</h2>
                    <p><strong>Inheritance</strong> allows a class to inherit members (properties, methods) from another class. The class being inherited from is called the <strong>base class</strong> (or parent), and the inheriting class is the <strong>derived class</strong> (or child).</p>

                    <div class="inheritance-diagram">
                        <div class="diagram-box base">
                            <div class="box-header">Animal (Base Class)</div>
                            <div class="box-content">
                                <span>Name</span>
                                <span>Age</span>
                                <span>Eat()</span>
                                <span>Sleep()</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-arrows">
                            <span>↙</span>
                            <span>↓</span>
                            <span>↘</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-derived">
                            <div class="diagram-box derived">
                                <div class="box-header">Dog</div>
                                <div class="box-content">
                                    <span>Bark()</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="diagram-box derived">
                                <div class="box-header">Cat</div>
                                <div class="box-content">
                                    <span>Meow()</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="diagram-box derived">
                                <div class="box-header">Bird</div>
                                <div class="box-content">
                                    <span>Fly()</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Base class
class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
    
    public void Eat()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} is eating.");
    }
    
    public void Sleep()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} is sleeping.");
    }
}

// Derived class - inherits from Animal using ':'
class Dog : Animal
{
    public string Breed { get; set; }
    
    public void Bark()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says: Woof!");
    }
}

// Usage
Dog dog = new Dog();
dog.Name = "Buddy";    // Inherited from Animal
dog.Age = 3;           // Inherited from Animal
dog.Breed = "Labrador"; // Defined in Dog

dog.Eat();   // Inherited: Buddy is eating.
dog.Sleep(); // Inherited: Buddy is sleeping.
dog.Bark();  // Dog-specific: Buddy says: Woof!</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The base Keyword</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>base</code> to access members of the base class, especially in constructors:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
    
    public Animal(string name, int age)
    {
        Name = name;
        Age = age;
    }
    
    public virtual void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some generic animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public string Breed { get; set; }
    
    // Call base class constructor
    public Dog(string name, int age, string breed) : base(name, age)
    {
        Breed = breed;
    }
    
    public void Bark()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says: Woof!");
    }
}

// Now we must provide all parameters
Dog dog = new Dog("Buddy", 3, "Labrador");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Method Overriding</h2>
                    <p>Derived classes can <strong>override</strong> methods from the base class to provide specialized behavior. The base method must be marked <code>virtual</code>, and the derived method uses <code>override</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    
    // Mark as virtual to allow overriding
    public virtual void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some generic animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    // Override the base method
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says: Woof! Woof!");
    }
}

class Cat : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says: Meow!");
    }
}

class Cow : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        // Can still call the base version if needed
        base.MakeSound();
        Console.WriteLine($"{Name} says: Moo!");
    }
}

// Usage
Dog dog = new Dog { Name = "Buddy" };
Cat cat = new Cat { Name = "Whiskers" };

dog.MakeSound();  // Buddy says: Woof! Woof!
cat.MakeSound();  // Whiskers says: Meow!</code></pre>

                    <div class="keyword-table">
                        <div class="keyword-row header">
                            <span>Keyword</span>
                            <span>Used In</span>
                            <span>Purpose</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="keyword-row">
                            <code>virtual</code>
                            <span>Base class</span>
                            <span>Allows method to be overridden</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="keyword-row">
                            <code>override</code>
                            <span>Derived class</span>
                            <span>Replaces the base implementation</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="keyword-row">
                            <code>base</code>
                            <span>Derived class</span>
                            <span>Accesses base class members</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Polymorphism</h2>
                    <p><strong>Polymorphism</strong> means "many forms." It allows you to treat objects of derived classes as objects of the base class:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// All animals can be stored as Animal type
Animal myPet = new Dog { Name = "Buddy" };
myPet.MakeSound();  // Buddy says: Woof! Woof! (calls Dog's version!)

// Array of different animals
Animal[] animals = new Animal[]
{
    new Dog { Name = "Buddy" },
    new Cat { Name = "Whiskers" },
    new Dog { Name = "Max" },
    new Cat { Name = "Luna" }
};

// Same method call, different behavior!
foreach (Animal animal in animals)
{
    animal.MakeSound();
}
// Output:
// Buddy says: Woof! Woof!
// Whiskers says: Meow!
// Max says: Woof! Woof!
// Luna says: Meow!</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Why Polymorphism Matters</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Polymorphism lets you write code that works with the base type but automatically uses the correct derived behavior. This makes your code more flexible and extensible.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Abstract Classes</h2>
                    <p>An <strong>abstract class</strong> cannot be instantiated directly. It serves as a base class and can contain abstract methods that derived classes must implement:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Abstract class - can't create instances directly
abstract class Shape
{
    public string Color { get; set; }
    
    // Abstract method - no implementation, MUST be overridden
    public abstract double GetArea();
    
    // Abstract property
    public abstract string ShapeName { get; }
    
    // Regular method - inherited as-is
    public void Describe()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"A {Color} {ShapeName} with area {GetArea():F2}");
    }
}

class Circle : Shape
{
    public double Radius { get; set; }
    
    public override string ShapeName =&gt; "Circle";
    
    public override double GetArea()
    {
        return Math.PI * Radius * Radius;
    }
}

class Rectangle : Shape
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }
    
    public override string ShapeName =&gt; "Rectangle";
    
    public override double GetArea()
    {
        return Width * Height;
    }
}

// Usage
// Shape shape = new Shape();  // Error! Can't instantiate abstract class

Circle circle = new Circle { Color = "Red", Radius = 5 };
Rectangle rect = new Rectangle { Color = "Blue", Width = 4, Height = 6 };

circle.Describe();  // A Red Circle with area 78.54
rect.Describe();    // A Blue Rectangle with area 24.00

// Polymorphism works with abstract classes too
Shape[] shapes = { circle, rect };
foreach (Shape shape in shapes)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{shape.ShapeName}: {shape.GetArea():F2}");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Interfaces</h2>
                    <p>An <strong>interface</strong> defines a contract that classes must follow. It specifies what a class must do, but not how:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Interface - defines a contract
interface IPlayable
{
    void Play();
    void Pause();
    void Stop();
    bool IsPlaying { get; }
}

interface IRecordable
{
    void Record();
    void StopRecording();
}

// Class implementing an interface
class MusicPlayer : IPlayable
{
    public bool IsPlaying { get; private set; }
    
    public void Play()
    {
        IsPlaying = true;
        Console.WriteLine("Music playing...");
    }
    
    public void Pause()
    {
        IsPlaying = false;
        Console.WriteLine("Music paused.");
    }
    
    public void Stop()
    {
        IsPlaying = false;
        Console.WriteLine("Music stopped.");
    }
}

// Class implementing multiple interfaces
class VideoRecorder : IPlayable, IRecordable
{
    public bool IsPlaying { get; private set; }
    public bool IsRecording { get; private set; }
    
    public void Play() { IsPlaying = true; }
    public void Pause() { IsPlaying = false; }
    public void Stop() { IsPlaying = false; }
    
    public void Record() { IsRecording = true; }
    public void StopRecording() { IsRecording = false; }
}</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Using interfaces for polymorphism
void StartMedia(IPlayable media)
{
    media.Play();
}

MusicPlayer mp3 = new MusicPlayer();
VideoRecorder vcr = new VideoRecorder();

StartMedia(mp3);  // Works!
StartMedia(vcr);  // Works too! Both implement IPlayable</code></pre>

                    <div class="comparison-box">
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Abstract Class</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Can have implementation</li>
                                <li>Can have fields</li>
                                <li>Single inheritance only</li>
                                <li>Use for "is-a" relationships</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-item">
                            <h4>Interface</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>No implementation (usually)</li>
                                <li>No fields (only properties)</li>
                                <li>Multiple implementation allowed</li>
                                <li>Use for "can-do" capabilities</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Sealed Classes</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>sealed</strong> class cannot be inherited from. Use it to prevent further derivation:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">sealed class FinalClass
{
    public void DoSomething() { }
}

// This would cause an error:
// class ChildClass : FinalClass { }  // Error! Cannot inherit from sealed class

// You can also seal specific methods
class Animal
{
    public virtual void MakeSound() { }
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    // Seal this method - no further overriding allowed
    public sealed override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}

class Poodle : Dog
{
    // Error! Cannot override sealed method
    // public override void MakeSound() { }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Type Checking and Casting</h2>
                    <p>When working with inheritance, you sometimes need to check types or cast objects:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">Animal animal = new Dog { Name = "Buddy" };

// 'is' keyword - check if object is of a type
if (animal is Dog)
{
    Console.WriteLine("It's a dog!");
}

// 'is' with pattern matching (modern C#)
if (animal is Dog dog)
{
    dog.Bark();  // Now we can use Dog-specific methods
}

// 'as' keyword - cast or return null
Dog? maybeDog = animal as Dog;
if (maybeDog != null)
{
    maybeDog.Bark();
}

// Direct cast (throws if wrong type)
Dog definitelyDog = (Dog)animal;
definitelyDog.Bark();

// Switch with type patterns
string GetAnimalInfo(Animal animal) =&gt; animal switch
{
    Dog d =&gt; $"Dog: {d.Name}, Breed: {d.Breed}",
    Cat c =&gt; $"Cat: {c.Name}",
    _ =&gt; $"Unknown animal: {animal.Name}"
};</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Complete Example</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Base class
abstract class Employee
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Id { get; set; }
    
    public Employee(string name, int id)
    {
        Name = name;
        Id = id;
    }
    
    // Abstract - each type calculates differently
    public abstract decimal CalculatePay();
    
    public virtual void DisplayInfo()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"ID: {Id}, Name: {Name}");
    }
}

// Salaried employee
class SalariedEmployee : Employee
{
    public decimal AnnualSalary { get; set; }
    
    public SalariedEmployee(string name, int id, decimal salary) 
        : base(name, id)
    {
        AnnualSalary = salary;
    }
    
    public override decimal CalculatePay()
    {
        return AnnualSalary / 12;  // Monthly pay
    }
    
    public override void DisplayInfo()
    {
        base.DisplayInfo();
        Console.WriteLine($"Type: Salaried, Annual: {AnnualSalary:C}");
    }
}

// Hourly employee
class HourlyEmployee : Employee
{
    public decimal HourlyRate { get; set; }
    public int HoursWorked { get; set; }
    
    public HourlyEmployee(string name, int id, decimal rate) 
        : base(name, id)
    {
        HourlyRate = rate;
    }
    
    public override decimal CalculatePay()
    {
        return HourlyRate * HoursWorked;
    }
    
    public override void DisplayInfo()
    {
        base.DisplayInfo();
        Console.WriteLine($"Type: Hourly, Rate: {HourlyRate:C}/hr");
    }
}

// Usage with polymorphism
List&lt;Employee&gt; employees = new()
{
    new SalariedEmployee("Alice", 1, 75000),
    new HourlyEmployee("Bob", 2, 25) { HoursWorked = 160 },
    new SalariedEmployee("Charlie", 3, 85000)
};

decimal totalPayroll = 0;
foreach (Employee emp in employees)
{
    emp.DisplayInfo();
    decimal pay = emp.CalculatePay();
    Console.WriteLine($"Pay: {pay:C}\n");
    totalPayroll += pay;
}
Console.WriteLine($"Total Payroll: {totalPayroll:C}");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Inheritance lets classes reuse and extend functionality from base classes.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>virtual</code> and <code>override</code> to customize inherited behavior.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Polymorphism lets you treat different types uniformly through a common base.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Abstract classes define contracts with some implementation; interfaces define pure contracts.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>is</code> and <code>as</code> for safe type checking and casting.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

